## 6. Directory Layout

Follows Go community conventions (`cmd/` entry point + `internal/` private packages):

```
{{PROJECT_NAME}}/
├── Dockerfile                      # Multi-stage: Go compile + frontend build (see Section 7)
├── backend/
│   ├── go.mod  go.sum
│   ├── cmd/
│   │   └── server/
│   │       └── main.go             # Entry point: wires up routing, pgx connection pool, static frontend, /health
│   ├── internal/
│   │   ├── config/                 # Config (reads env vars)
│   │   ├── db/                     # pgxpool connection pool
│   │   ├── repo/                   # Hand-written pgx repositories (one file per entity, typed queries)
│   │   ├── handlers/               # REST handlers (corresponding to Section 5), one file per resource
│   │   ├── service/                # Business logic
│   │   ├── model/                  # Domain structs
│   │   └── middleware/             # Optimistic-lock conflict -> 409, JSON errors, request logging
│   ├── migrations/                 # golang-migrate plain SQL
│   └── scripts/                    # Test scripts etc.
└── frontend/                       # If needed: React + TS + Vite
    ├── src/
    │   ├── pages/
    │   ├── components/
    │   ├── api/                    # REST client
    │   └── App.tsx
    ├── package.json
    └── vite.config.ts
```

## 7. Deployment

- Shape: single container (port 8080, `/health` returns `ok`); frontend build output is served statically by the Go process (`embed.FS` or a static directory)
- Build: Docker multi-stage — `node:20-alpine` builds the frontend → `golang:1.25-alpine` compiles (`CGO_ENABLED=0`, embeds the frontend dist) → `alpine:3.20` minimal runtime image
- Horizontal scaling: Go instances are stateless and can run multiple replicas sharing the same database

